      [
        "Performance Features",
        "1. Classified by the quantity of hoisting steel ropes: single‑rope cage and multi‑rope cage. Single‑rope cages are generally applied for shafts within 400 m depth; multi‑rope cages are normally used for shafts deeper than 350 m.\n2. Classified by deck quantity: single‑deck, double‑deck and multi‑deck cages.\n3. Classified by guide type: rope‑guided cage and rigid‑guided cage. Rigid‑guided cages are further divided into steel‑rail guide type and composite‑steel guide type.\n4. Classified by matched mine car capacity: 0.5 T, 1 T, 1.5 T and 3 T mine‑car cages.\n5. The cage body for both single‑rope and multi‑rope cages is assembled by three‑section vertical side panels connected via cross beams. Two main structural forms are available: flat‑steel truss type and frame type, among which frame type is more widely adopted. Three connection methods are applied for cage joints: riveting, bolting and welding.",
        "",
        "",
        "",
        "",
        "",
        ""
      ],
      [
        "Working Principle",
        "As a mine hoisting container, the mine cage usually carries loads of several tons. Its operating principle is similar to an elevator, and reliable safety and running performance must be guaranteed.",
